# Detached CPU training

Local CPU / tabular Q training must **never** be tied to an interactive Cursor terminal or the Flask process lifetime.

## Prefer `/rl` Start

- Start CPU training from the RL page (`POST /api/rl/control/start` with Device=CPU).
- `rl_control._start_local` already spawns `parallel_train.py` **detached** (Windows: `DETACHED_PROCESS` + new process group; Unix: `start_new_session`), logs to `backend/RL/output/local_train.log`, pid in `local_train.pid`.
- Restarting Flask or closing the web terminal must not interrupt an active CPU run. Stop only via `/rl` Stop (or `taskkill` on that pid).

## If launching from a shell

- Do **not** run `parallel_train.py` in the foreground of a terminal you may restart.
- Use a detached spawn (same flags as `_start_local`) or `block_until_ms: 0` only after confirming the process is written to `local_train.pid` and will outlive the shell.
- Never Ctrl+C a shell that is the training process itself.

## Code changes while training

- Safe: edit frontend, notebooks, docs, most backend modules Flask will reload.
- Unsafe without Stop: replacing/rewriting files the live trainer has imported in-process (`parallel_train.py`, `agents.py` Q path, etc.) mid-run — the running process keeps the old code in memory; a Stop/Start is required to pick up trainer logic changes.
